{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatReflection"],"declarations":11,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.bruhatLe_iff_reflLe_An","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.bruhatLe_iff_reflLe_An","kind":"theorem","line":233,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L233-L238","doc":"**The two orders coincide on type `A`**, with no hypothesis — the symmetric group, hence the\nform a consumer of Mathas / Dipper–James works in. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.reflLe_of_bruhatLe_An","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.reflLe_of_bruhatLe_An","kind":"theorem","line":228,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L228-L231","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.bruhatLe_iff_leftReflLe","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.bruhatLe_iff_leftReflLe","kind":"theorem","line":221,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L221-L224","doc":"**The subword order and the LEFT-handed reflection order also coincide.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.bruhatLe_of_leftReflLe","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.bruhatLe_of_leftReflLe","kind":"theorem","line":215,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L215-L219","doc":"`LeftReflLe → BruhatLe` in general — the generic form of\n`BruhatReflection.bruhatLe_of_leftReflLe_An`, which was stated only on type `A`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.leftReflLe_of_bruhatLe","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.leftReflLe_of_bruhatLe","kind":"theorem","line":209,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L209-L213","doc":"**`BruhatLe → LeftReflLe`** — the direction `BruhatReflection` recorded as blocked. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.leftReflLe_inv_of_reflLe","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.leftReflLe_inv_of_reflLe","kind":"theorem","line":201,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L201-L207","doc":"The chain version. As on the right-handed side, the chain does **not** reverse: `⁻¹` is applied\npointwise and `ReflTransGen` is closed under that. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.leftReflStep_inv_of_reflStep","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.leftReflStep_inv_of_reflStep","kind":"theorem","line":192,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L192-L199","doc":"A right step inverts to a left step — the mirror of\n`BruhatReflection.reflStep_inv_of_leftReflStep`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.bruhatLe_iff_reflLe","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.bruhatLe_iff_reflLe","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L175-L181","doc":"**The two Bruhat orders are the same relation.**\n\nThe corpus's subword `BruhatLe` and the reflection-generated `ReflLe` of Mathas / Dipper–James agree\non the nose, so a statement proved against either may be cited for the other. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.reflLe_of_bruhatLe","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.reflLe_of_bruhatLe","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L170-L173","doc":"**`BruhatLe → ReflLe`.** The converse bridge, closed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.reflLe_mul_simple_of_ascent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.reflLe_mul_simple_of_ascent","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L75-L109","doc":"**The upward lifting property for `≤ᵣ`** — the input `BruhatReflection` isolated and could not\nsupply.\n\n`x ≤ᵣ y` with `s` a right *ascent* of `y` gives `x·s ≤ᵣ y·s`, provided the converse is already known\nbelow `N` and `ℓ(y) < N`.\n\nNote there is **no hypothesis that `s` is an ascent of `x`**: the first draft carried one and the\ncompiler reported it unused. In the `refl` case `x = y` supplies it, and neither the ascent nor the\ndescent case of the chain refers to `x`'s own length. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.ConverseBelow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IwahoriHecke.BruhatConverse.ConverseBelow","kind":"def","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/IwahoriHecke/BruhatConverse.lean#L67-L71","doc":"The converse, restricted to elements shorter than `N`. This is the outer induction hypothesis,\nnamed so that the upward-lifting lemma can take it as an argument — which is exactly what makes its\ndescent case go through. ","signatureChunk":0}]}